    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(7))  # F0..F6\n    customers = list(range(8))  # C0..C7\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(1, 93), (48, 34), (26, 51), (20, 37), (90, 64), (58, 20), (67, 5)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(85, 10), (34, 25), (87, 4), (82, 0), (59, 1), (35, 33), (2, 32), (5, 23)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [85.0, 104.0, 192.0, 105.0, 188.0, 132.0, 78.0]\n    customer_demands = [27.0, 10.0, 48.0, 30.0, 46.0, 30.0, 46.0, 14.0]\n    fixed_costs = [173.0, 487.0, 351.0, 261.0, 478.0, 158.0, 192.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse location and customer service plan to minimize total cost\nDate: [Today]\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point for our distribution footprint. We have seven candidate warehouse locations and eight customer locations, and we need to choose which warehouses to open and how to ship product so that every customer is served at the lowest possible total cost. The candidate warehouse coordinates are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (1, 93)\n  - F1: (48, 34)\n  - F2: (26, 51)\n  - F3: (20, 37)\n  - F4: (90, 64)\n  - F5: (58, 20)\n  - F6: (67, 5), and the customer delivery points are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (85, 10)\n  - C1: (34, 25)\n  - C2: (87, 4)\n  - C3: (82, 0)\n  - C4: (59, 1)\n  - C5: (35, 33)\n  - C6: (2, 32)\n  - C7: (5, 23). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, and these will be used to determine shipping mileage.\n\nOpening any warehouse carries a fixed cost per site, detailed in [173.0, 487.0, 351.0, 261.0, 478.0, 158.0, 192.0]. Shipping expense scales with both distance and volume at 0.5 per kilometer per unit shipped. A facility can only ship if it is opened.\n\nEach warehouse has a throughput ceiling captured in [85.0, 104.0, 192.0, 105.0, 188.0, 132.0, 78.0], and each customer has demand that must be fully satisfied as specified in [27.0, 10.0, 48.0, 30.0, 46.0, 30.0, 46.0, 14.0]. Our plan must respect these limits while meeting all demand.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize the sum of facility opening costs and transportation spend. The practical trade-off is familiar\u2014opening more warehouses can shorten shipping distances (and reduce variable costs) but increases fixed costs, while relying on fewer warehouses saves on openings but may require longer hauls and risks hitting capacity constraints.\n\nThe decision deliverable will specify:\n- Which of the seven candidate warehouses to open\n- How to allocate customer shipments to the open sites so that all demand is met within capacity limits\n- The resulting total cost and rationale, using Euclidean distances from the provided coordinates to compute shipping mileage\n\nOnce approved, we will finalize the network configuration and its implementation timeline based on the selected sites and shipment plan.",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse location and customer service plan to minimize total cost\nDate: [Today]\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point for our distribution footprint. We have seven candidate warehouse locations and eight customer locations, and we need to choose which warehouses to open and how to ship product so that every customer is served at the lowest possible total cost. The candidate warehouse coordinates are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer delivery points are provided in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, and these will be used to determine shipping mileage.\n\nOpening any warehouse carries a fixed cost per site, detailed in {FIXED_COSTS}. Shipping expense scales with both distance and volume at {TRANSPORT_RATE} per kilometer per unit shipped. A facility can only ship if it is opened.\n\nEach warehouse has a throughput ceiling captured in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer has demand that must be fully satisfied as specified in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Our plan must respect these limits while meeting all demand.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize the sum of facility opening costs and transportation spend. The practical trade-off is familiar\u2014opening more warehouses can shorten shipping distances (and reduce variable costs) but increases fixed costs, while relying on fewer warehouses saves on openings but may require longer hauls and risks hitting capacity constraints.\n\nThe decision deliverable will specify:\n- Which of the seven candidate warehouses to open\n- How to allocate customer shipments to the open sites so that all demand is met within capacity limits\n- The resulting total cost and rationale, using Euclidean distances from the provided coordinates to compute shipping mileage\n\nOnce approved, we will finalize the network configuration and its implementation timeline based on the selected sites and shipment plan."
